import json
H = "14. Addendum: analysis plans for H4b, H8, H9a and Study 2 (fixed 22 October 2026, before the data they use were examined)"
P = [
("p", "These plans were written after OSF reopened and the Michalopoulos and Xue (2021) replication archive was obtained, but before any folklore score, adoption code, ethnographic valence code or new H8 model had been computed. They inherit Deviations D1 to D13: coders are instances of one language model, and every result is exploratory. A copy of the protocol as it stood when this addendum was added is frozen in Drive as 'FROZEN 2026-10-22 Narrative Selection and Societal Endurance: Registered Report Protocol (addendum, pre-analysis)'."),
("h", "14.1 Study 2: folklore content and trust"),
("p", "Data: the replication archive of Michalopoulos and Xue (2021; Harvard Dataverse, doi:10.7910/DVN/IXOHKB): Berezkin's 2,564 motifs with his own English titles and descriptions, motif incidence across 1,245 Ethnographic Atlas groups and 199 countries, and their outcome files (country trust, European Social Survey second-generation immigrants, Ethnographic Atlas variables)."),
("p", "Measure: each motif's English title and description are scored with MAC-D under the rules of the corpus specification (Section 3 of corpus_spec.md). A motif carries domain d if it contains at least one virtue term of d. For each group or country, the domain score is the share of its motifs carrying d (incidence-weighted for countries). The folklore CCI is the mean of the seven z-scored domain shares, with z computed across the units of each dataset; the net index uses virtue minus vice flags. Units with fewer than 5 motifs are excluded."),
("p", "Models: (S2-1) country trust, ln(average WVS trust) on CCI with the documentation controls of Michalopoulos and Xue (log first publication year, log number of sources), robust errors, without and with continent dummies; secondary outcome GPS trust. (S2-2) second-generation immigrants in the European Social Survey: ln(trust) on the CCI of the parents' origin country with their individual controls and host-country-by-round fixed effects, errors clustered by origin country, on their exact sample. (S2-3) political scale in the Ethnographic Atlas: levels of jurisdictional hierarchy above the local community (v33) on CCI with the documentation controls and continent fixed effects, errors clustered by language family, groups with more than 4 motifs; then adding high-god dummies (v34) as the Study 2 analogue of H5. Positive control: the authors' trickster-punishment measure is re-estimated in S2-1 and S2-2 with the same code; if it does not reproduce their published sign and approximate size, the pipeline is treated as faulty and the CCI results are not interpreted. Prediction: positive CCI coefficients. Smallest effect of interest: 0.10 SD of the outcome per SD of CCI; equivalence by two one-sided tests at 90 percent."),
("h", "14.2 H4b: reverse transmission"),
("p", "Frame: Study 1 polities coded as conquered (T2) whose narrative tradition has a CCI score (117 episodes). Each episode is coded by independent model instances, blind to CCI values: the conquerors' tradition at the time of conquest (one of the 15 scored traditions, another named tradition, or unknown); whether, within 100 years, the conquering ruling group adopted the conquered population's tradition as its own (yes, partial, no, unknown); and whether the conquering elite used writing at the time of conquest. Episodes where conquerors already shared the conquered tradition are excluded, since no adoption is possible. A random 20 percent is coded twice and disagreements are adjudicated."),
("p", "Analysis: logistic regression of adoption (yes = 1, no = 0; partial excluded, and counted as yes in a sensitivity analysis) on the conquered tradition's CCI (z-scored in the H4b sample), controlling for the conquered polity's social complexity (prestige) and writing, and the conquerors' writing; errors clustered by conquered tradition, with a tradition-level permutation test as the primary inference. Secondary: where both traditions are scored, adoption on the CCI difference (conquered minus conqueror), the two-alternative form of conditional logit. Prediction: odds ratio above 1. Falsified if the odds ratio is below 1 or its 90 percent interval lies within 0.80 to 1.25."),
("h", "14.3 H9a: moral valence of the seven cooperative behaviours in a new sample"),
("p", "Sample: one society per top-level Glottolog language family, excluding the 60 Probability Sample File societies used by Curry, Mullins and Whitehouse (2019), drawn from societies with a full English-language ethnography published before 1929 and available as public-domain full text. Target 30 societies. The candidate list and the final sample are fixed and recorded before any passage is coded."),
("p", "Passages: each ethnography is cleaned as in the corpus specification and split into paragraphs. For each MAC-D domain, paragraphs containing at least one virtue or vice term of that domain are candidates, and up to 12 per domain per society are drawn at random (seed 20261022). Using the dictionary's own term lists, including its vice terms, avoids hand-picked keywords and lets disapproval surface."),
("p", "Coding: independent model instances, not told the hypothesis, code each paragraph: whether it describes members of the society performing, expecting or evaluating the domain's behaviour (helping kin, loyalty to the group, reciprocity, bravery, deference to superiors, fair division, respect for property); and if so, the society's valence toward it (positive: approved, praised, obligatory; negative: condemned, punished, shameful; none stated), with the evaluative words quoted. A random 20 percent is coded twice. Criterion (Section 2): H9a is falsified for a behaviour if it is judged bad in more than 5 percent of the instances in which its valence is coded; each percentage is reported with a 95 percent Wilson interval."),
("h", "14.4 H8: stronger tests with existing data"),
("p", "(H8-1) Internal-collapse hazard by moralizing enforcement of rulers within Natural Geographic Areas: cause-specific Cox model stratified by home NGA, so that polities are compared only with others in the same region, adjusted for social complexity, population and era. (H8-2) The same model restricted to literate states (writing present). (H8-3) Crisis severity across all 169 CrisisDB crises, not only polity-ending ones: count of civil war, extermination and collapse present, by moralizing enforcement, with errors clustered by polity, adjusted for population and era; and the probability that a crisis ends with the ruler deposed or assassinated. Predictions: hazard ratio below 1 (H8-1, H8-2) and lower severity and deposition (H8-3). The narrative Ruler Constraint item and an independent measure of accountable rule (for example the Blanton and Fargher codes for 30 premodern states, which are not public) remain unavailable."),
]
json.dump(dict(H=H, P=P), open('/root/analysis/out/addendum_text.json','w'))
t = H + ' '.join(x for _, x in P); assert '—' not in t and '–' not in t; print(len(t))
